`@NotFound(action = NotFoundAction.IGNORE)` has the side effect of making a **to-one association non-lazy**, even when it is declared with `FetchType.LAZY`. Hibernate treats an orphaned foreign-key value as if it were `null` rather than throwing an exception.  

For example:

```java
@ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
@NotFound(action = NotFoundAction.IGNORE)
private SomeEntity association;
```

The association will still be loaded non-lazily. Hibernate may therefore issue additional queries, and its behavior can vary depending on whether the association is loaded with `JOIN` or `SELECT` fetching. (Hibernate `@NotFound` Javadoc; Hibernate developers’ discussion.)
